# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — agent harness

{{REPO_TABLE}}

## Four records

`docs/agile/` is **what we are doing**. `docs/knowledge/` is **what we know
about the product**. `docs/lessons/` is **how work goes wrong here** — one
imperative line per rule, filed when a ticket bounced off a gate. `forge.json`
is **what this project is shaped like** — its repositories, scopes, owning
agents and runnable checks.

The first three are plain Markdown committed here, each with a generated
`INDEX.md` you must never edit and a validator that exits 2 when it drifts. The
fourth is read, never assumed:

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py scopes
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py checks <scope>
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py doctor
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py gates          # the six gates and their contracts
```

## Work goes through the tracker

Read [`docs/agile/README.md`](docs/agile/README.md) before starting anything;
[`docs/agile/SCHEMA.md`](docs/agile/SCHEMA.md) is the specification.

```
/agile:board            what is going on
/agile:groom <request>  turn a request into tickets
/agile:next             what to work on
/agile:work <ID>        the six gates: spec → tests → implement → validate → docs → board
/agile:close <ID>       merge, record the sha, close
/agile:bug <symptom>    file and triage a defect
```

## Knowledge goes through the knowledge base

```
docs/knowledge/<scope>/<type>/{<type>} <description> - <yyyy-mm-dd>.md
```

`<type>` is one of `business-rule`, `troubleshooting`, `overview`,
`integration`, `api-contract`, `decision`, `runbook`, `data-model`. **The braces
around the type are literal**, the description is lowercase words separated by
spaces, and the created date follows a ` - ` separator. Paths therefore need
quoting — let `kb.py` print them rather than retyping. The specification is
[`docs/knowledge/README.md`](docs/knowledge/README.md); the procedure the agents
follow is `.claude/skills/project-knowledge/SKILL.md`.

```
/kb:consult <subject>  what do we already know?
/kb:new <finding>      file it, correctly typed and cross-linked
/kb:audit              gaps, misfilings, staleness, broken links
/kb:lint               validate the knowledge base and the tracker's links into it

python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py find <keyword> [...]     # search, exit 2 = a gap
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py for-ticket <TICKET-ID>   # the ticket's reading list
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py new <type> <scope> "<description>" --ticket <ID>
```

**Consulting the knowledge base is a step of the cycle, not optional reading.**
Before changing business logic, an integration, a domain workflow or the
architecture, search it. After the work reveals something durable, file it — one
finding per note, under the right type, cross-linked in `## Related` in both
directions. Where a note and the code disagree, **the code wins**: say so, and
fix the note.
{{KB_CALLOUT}}
## Lessons are what the gates taught

```
/lessons:new <finding>  record what a bounced gate taught, as one imperative line
/lessons:retro          merge duplicates, retire what no longer bites, stay inside the budget
/lessons:lint           validate the layer and the tracker's links into it

python3 .claude/scripts/lessons.py for <agent>   # the rules that bind it, capped and ranked
```

Each rule is one line of at most 120 characters, and the hand-off is capped at
`policy.lesson_budget` of them so that reading them stays cheaper than
rediscovering them — `agile.py handoff` renders that capped list straight into
every dispatch. A ticket that **bounced** owes one, filed by whoever took that
edge at the moment they took it. Confirming an existing rule
(`lessons.py confirm <ID> --ticket <ID>`) counts, and beats a near-duplicate.
The specification is [`docs/lessons/README.md`](docs/lessons/README.md); the
procedure is `.claude/skills/agile-lessons/SKILL.md`.

Agents: {{AGENT_LIST}} in `.claude/agents/`. Their operating rules live in
`.claude/skills/agile-{artifacts,claims,grooming,git,dod,lessons}/` and
`.claude/skills/project-knowledge/`.

## Dispatch is built and checked, never written

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py handoff <ID> --gate <N>    # what the subagent is told
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py handback <ID> --gate <N>   # what actually came back
```

`handoff` produces the whole prompt: binding instructions first, repeated last,
with reference material in between as paths and commands rather than pasted
files. Send it verbatim and **send nothing else**. A fact that reaches a
subagent only through your own message survives exactly as long as your context
does; written into the ticket, the spec or the lessons layer it is in git and
the next payload renders it back. If you want to add a paragraph, the paragraph
belongs in a file.

The one thing the repository cannot reconstruct is what the user said. The
moment they answer gate 1's questions, write it down:

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py spec answer <ID> Q1="<what the user said>"
```

`handback` then checks each gate against the files rather than against the
subagent's report — status, branch, PR, evidence per requirement, lint. Exit 2
means the gate did not land whatever the report said.

## Rules that are not negotiable

- **Every `INDEX.md` is generated.** Never edit `docs/agile/INDEX.md`,
  `docs/knowledge/INDEX.md` or `docs/lessons/INDEX.md`. Change the ticket, the
  note or the lesson and let the `index` commands regenerate them. A hook denies
  the write anyway.
- **Every task and bug carries `spec:` and `docs:`.** A `PreToolUse` hook
  refuses a new ticket missing either, because those two fields are what make
  the grilling step and the consult step happen at all. Each has a waiver —
  `spec_waiver: NO-SPEC (<reason>)`, `docs_waiver: NO-DOCS (<reason>)` — and a
  waiver is a claim somebody can disagree with, never a shortcut.
- **A note is created by `kb.py new`, never by hand.** A `PreToolUse` hook
  refuses a misfiled or misnamed file under `docs/knowledge/`. `overview` is not
  the default type — a rule is a `business-rule`, an incident is
  `troubleshooting`, an external system is an `integration`.
- **A ticket says what it taught before it closes.** `lessons:` names the rule
  this ticket produced or confirmed, or `lessons_waiver: NO-LESSON (<reason>)`
  says it taught nothing. The waiver is normal for a ticket that ran straight
  through, and a rejection for one that bounced: that bounce is the only
  evidence this harness produces that the *process* failed rather than the code.
- **A task's `scope` is exactly one** of {{SCOPE_LIST}}. Anything spanning two
  is two tasks under one story.
- **One active ticket per working tree**, counting `writing_tests` as active.
  `speccing` does not occupy one — it cuts no branch and writes only the tracker,
  so the next ticket can be specified while this one is implemented.
- **The cycle is specification-first, then test-first.** There is no
  `todo -> writing_tests` and no `todo -> in_progress`. `qa` claims out of
  `todo`, grills the request into a numbered brief, and proves a test red per
  requirement before an engineer starts.
- **QA does not guess, and does not ask.** What the knowledge base and the code
  cannot answer becomes a batch of questions that **the top-level session** puts
  to the user, in one go, in the user's language. An unanswered question becomes
  carryover — a recorded decision — never an assumption.
- **Nobody closes their own work.** An engineer stops at `review`, QA moves it to
  `verify`, `verify` means awaiting the user's acceptance, and only the top-level
  session sets `done` inside `/agile:close`.
- **An unavailable check is `SKIPPED (<reason> — see TASK-000X)`, never a pass.**
  The matrix is `python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py checks <scope>`.
- **Every code commit carries a `Refs: <TICKET-ID>` trailer.** Conventional
  Commits, third-person verb: `feat: adds price per day to the subscription
  resource`.
- Never commit anything on the project's never-commit list (`git.never_commit`
  in `forge.json`). Never push with `--no-verify`.

## Verify your work before handing it back

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py lint     # exit 2 means the tracker is inconsistent
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py lint        # exit 2 means the knowledge base is inconsistent
python3 .claude/scripts/lessons.py lint   # exit 2 means a rule drifted, duplicated or went stale
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py doctor   # exit 2 means the harness config drifted
```
